sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch11-19> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The shell and the aptychus of ammonites are probably the ventral and dorsal portions of a once united conical shell split by unequal growth, as in pteropods; other cephalopods retain only one of the two, the ventral one as the shell of Nautilus and the dorsal one as the shell of Sepia or Spirula.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Ammonite shell and aptychus are two parts of a once united split cone";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XI, “The Logarithmic Spiral”; topic “Pteropod shells and cephalopod shells”. Scope: ammonoids, Nautilus, Sepia, Spirula and other cephalopods; thecosome pteropods"
}
